  "summary": "Tsujigahana (辻ヶ花) is a Japanese fabric dyeing technique that originated in the Muromachi period.",
  "description": "Tsujigahana (辻ヶ花) is a Japanese fabric dyeing technique that originated in the Muromachi period.",
  "culturalContext": "The name tsujigahana translates to \"flowers at the crossroads\". Documents from the Muromachi and Momoyama periods use the term to describe garments, but are unclear about whether the term refers to color, motifs, or dyeing methods. During the 20th century, scholars redefined tsujigahana to refer to a specific textile technique that uses stitch-resist dyeing (nuishime shibori) and ink painting to decorate a plain weave, lightweight fabric, often silk. The fabric is often further embellished using embroidery and gold leaf.",
